 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Displacement General Setup](11527-displacement-general-setup.md)
-Displacement General Setup is a rendering technique that adds geometric detail to volumetric simulations by sampling a texture map and shifting fluid or surface points in a direction determined by that texture. It solves the limitation of static volumetric representations by allowing interactive deformation without mesh re-tessellation, building on the principles of [Displacement Mapping for Volume Rendering](10659-displacement-mapping-for-volume-rendering.md). Unlike standard scalar displacement which often produces symmetrical deformations, this setup supports vector-based and surface-normal-based shifts, enabling complex, asymmetric geometries such as waves with convex backs and concave fronts.
+A rendering technique that adds geometric detail to volumetric simulations by sampling a texture map and shifting fluid or surface points in a direction determined by that texture. It solves the limitation of static volumetric representations by allowing interactive deformation without mesh re-tessellation, building on the principles of [Displacement Mapping for Volume Rendering](10659-displacement-mapping-for-volume-rendering.md). Unlike standard scalar displacement which often produces symmetrical deformations, this setup supports vector-based and surface-normal-based shifts, enabling complex, asymmetric geometries such as waves with convex backs and concave fronts.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the cost of 3D texture lookups for both the volume and displacement fields, plus the arithmetic for coordinate transformation. No mesh traversal or re-tessellation is required.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the original volume texture and the displacement vector texture [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Handles discontinuous and unorthogonal displacements (cuts, peeling) without mesh re-tessellation; supports interactive deformation; works with implicit surfaces or scalar volumes [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires correct normal calculation to avoid lighting artifacts at discontinuities [S1, unofficial]. Performance depends on the resolution of the displacement texture.
-*   **When to use:** When rendering volumetric objects that undergo complex, discontinuous deformations (e.g., cutting, peeling) and explicit surface meshes are not available or desirable [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the volume rendering algorithm (e.g., ray marching) and the cost of texture lookups for both the proxy and displacement maps. The inverse mapping adds one extra 3D texture lookup per sample.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the proxy volume, the original volume, and the displacement texture. Displacement textures are typically 8-bit or 16-bit [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Allows interactive deformation (cuts, peeling) without mesh re-tessellation; supports unorthogonal and discontinuous displacements; works with implicit surfaces, level sets, and volume textures [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires correct normal calculation to avoid artifacts at discontinuities; proxy geometry must bound the displaced object accurately; not suitable for methods requiring explicit surface meshes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When simulating dynamic, discontinuous volumetric effects like cutting or peeling on GPU hardware without explicit mesh structures [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When high-fidelity surface continuity is required without complex normal computation, or when the displacement is purely cosmetic on a static surface (standard displacement mapping may be simpler).
 
 ## Background
-Displacement mapping is commonly used for adding surface details to an object. This paper outlines a generalized notion of displacement mapping, which allows for unconventional features such as unorthogonal and discontinuous displacement [S1, unofficial]. By lifting the restriction on the geometric properties of the displacement, many different special effects including peeling, cutting and deforming an object can be generated [S1, unofficial]. These types of operations are useful for volumetric objects, where the interior of objects is represented [S1, unofficial]. To address the technical difficulties associated with this generalization, inverse displacement maps in 3D vector space were employed, and a collection of techniques including sampling displaced objects through a proxy geometry and computing displaced surface normals was devised [S1, unofficial].
+Displacement mapping is commonly used for adding surface details to an object. This method outlines a generalized notion of displacement mapping, allowing for unconventional features such as unorthogonal and discontinuous displacement [S1, unofficial]. By lifting the restriction on the geometric properties of the displacement, it generates special effects including peeling, cutting, and deforming objects, which are useful for volumetric objects where the interior is represented [S1, unofficial]. To address technical difficulties, it employs inverse displacement maps in 3D vector space and devises techniques including sampling displaced objects through proxy geometry and computing displaced surface normals [S1, unofficial]. The approach was demonstrated on current GPU hardware, adapting slicing-based rendering strategies [S1, unofficial].
 
